  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and scattered thunderstorms are expected \nahead of a cold front through tonight. A few of these storms may \nbecome severe with damaging wind gusts being the primary hazard, \nespecially for areas across the western Southern Tier.\n\nA deep low centered over the Hudson Bay at 15Z this morning is \ncurrently supporting a warm front across western Ontario into \neastern New York and a cold front across upper Lake Michigan into \nsoutheastern Wisconsin and further southwest into the Plains. These \nfronts are currently responsible for a few areas of rain and \nthunderstorms. The widespread showers across much of western and \nnorth central New York this morning are from the passage of the warm \nfront lifting northeast. Meanwhile a line of showers and \nthunderstorms ahead of the cold front are passing east across the \nOntario peninsula, Michigan and into Chicago as of 17Z. In between \nthe two features, much of the area remains sodden with cloud cover, \nhowever across the far western Southern Tier there is a patch of \nclearing, especially along the New York and Pennsylvania state line.\n\nAhead of the incoming cold front, a prefrontal surface trough will \ncross the region this afternoon, causing 0-6km shear to topple out \nat around 40 kt. This combined with low level convergence, and the \nlimited instability of up to 1000 J/kg this afternoon and evening \nwill support a few embedded thunderstorms to pass across WNY in time \nfor the evening commute. A few of these storms may have the \npotential to become severe producing damaging wind gusts, however \ngiven the overlap in the peak of surface based CAPE and increasing \nstorm relative helicity a tornado can't be ruled out. Please note, \ndue to the limited amount of clearing this afternoon the best \nchances for severe thunderstorms will lie across Chautauqua, \nCattaraugus, and Allegany counties.\n\nOtherwise, outside of the severe weather potential a plume of \nprecipitable water values of 1.8 inches (99th percentile for early \nSeptember) will pass across the region through this evening, which \nwill bring the potential for heavy downpours. In addition to the \nhigher precipitable water amounts, there may be some training of \nshowers/storms along the frontal boundary which will support a low \nrisk for flooding across the western Southern Tier to the eastern \nLake Ontario region.\n\nShowers will taper off from northwest to southeast overnight as the \nfront passes across the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Near to slightly below normal temperatures with dry \nweather to return for the end of the work week.\n\nSurface high pressure will build into the region late Thursday \nthrough Friday, supporting dry weather through the start of the \nweekend. Additionally, in the wake of the passing front Thursday, a \ncooler airmass will slide across the region for the end of the week, \nsupporting near to slightly below normal temperatures.",
